[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports show two bugs, and they
ended differently. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October.

<Callout kind="warn">
Only the parser bug was fixed. The hint bug was closed as won't fix, because the booking-v3 form is
retired. "Fixed" is true of one ticket, not of #4471.
</Callout>

## What the reports show

The table is built from the ticket export, at render time. It counts each report once, because the
export repeats some rows, and it places each report by its tenant.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The last column of the table was written before these tickets existed. Each cause now has one:

- `parser`: [issue #1](https://github.com/Wozacosta/what-it-shows/issues/1), fixed by
  `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. The shared parser read every date month first, so a
  day-first tenant's dates came out flipped.
- `hint`: [issue #2](https://github.com/Wozacosta/what-it-shows/issues/2), won't fix: booking-v3
  form retired. The form printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People typed what it told them,
  and the form read the date the tenant's way.

## How to tell them apart

Ask where the report came from, not what it says. A report is `hint` when it came in through the
form of a day-first tenant on booking-v3. Every other report in the ticket is `parser`.

The symptom cannot separate them: both end with a flipped date. Neither can the subject line, which
is all that [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) reads. Neither can the intake tag.
Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, so an en-US browser at a day-first tenant gets tagged
`hint` whichever way the report came in. Compare the "tagged as" column with the cause.

## What does not fit

The note under the table counts reports that read as a one-day move, not a flip. One client in
Lisbon and one in Toronto saw different weekdays for the same meeting, and a month-first parser
does not do that. They stay counted under `parser`, the way the desk files them, because they come
from those tenants. Nobody has shown that the parser caused them, so the parser fix may not cover them
([issue #1](https://github.com/Wozacosta/what-it-shows/issues/1) lists them). If they keep coming,
they need a ticket of their own.

